window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-11-27T05:00:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-11-26","event":"Printed: Tamura speech and press conference (rate to neutral soon, 25 bp every few months base case, 50 bp possible, December \"or later\"); September leading index revised to 116.5 from 116.4, coincident 119.5 unchanged; MoF primary-dealer meeting on 2027 issuance (increases and decreases by maturity requested; no readout); Kihara protest on Section 301; JGBs: 2-year 1.740%, 30-year 3.895%; Nikkei 57,499.16 (+0.86%)","why":"Tamura set the hawkish marker and the dealer meeting the super-long issuance tone; both are now in the price."},{"date":"2026-11-27","event":"Printed: Tokyo CPI (Nov, 08:30): core 2.9% from 2.6% (consensus 3.0%), headline 2.8%, core-core 2.5%; Nikkei 58,172.04 (+1.17%) at the morning close. Pending: liquidity supply auction (5 to 11 years, ¥500bn, 12:35); MoF investor meeting; US half-session (13:00 ET); month-end London fix 01:00 JST","why":"The early read for the 18 Dec national CPI printed above target on energy with the underlying pace easing; the afternoon close against 60,030 and 3,895 and the investor meeting readout are what remain."},{"date":"2026-11-30","event":"2-year JGB auction (¥2.8tn); Ueda speech and press conference (Nagoya); industrial production prelim (Oct; METI survey +5.1% m/m); retail sales (Oct); unemployment and jobs ratio (Oct); housing starts (Oct); MoF intervention total (Nov)","why":"Ueda's last scheduled words before the December meeting; the October hard data test the household leg; the intervention total shows whether the MoF acted near 160."},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"Manufacturing PMI final (Nov; flash 53.7); Q3 MoF corporate survey (capex); 10-year JGB auction; Huang testifies to the House","why":"Confirms the manufacturing leg of the regime and the capex input to the GDP second estimate; the Nvidia governance strand drives the Nikkei leaders."},{"date":"2026-12-02","event":"Consumer confidence (Nov; October 35.0)","why":"A print under 35 is the Slowdown trigger on the household leg."},{"date":"2026-12-03","event":"Services PMI final (Nov; flash 50.5); Huang testifies to the Senate","why":"A services final under 50 moves the regime to Slowdown."},{"date":"2026-12-08","event":"Q3 GDP second estimate (08:50; first −0.1% annualized); wages and real wages (Oct); household spending (Oct); US FOMC starts (8–9 Dec, 97.8% for 25 bp); 30-year JGB auction (MUFG lists 8 Dec; previously 3 Dec)","why":"A second negative quarter on the revision is the Slowdown trigger; real wages are the virtuous-cycle test. The 30-year auction is the super-long demand test after the 40-year cleared on 2.63 cover."},{"date":"2026-12-12","event":"Diet session ends; food consumption-tax cut bill due to pass the Upper House by this date","why":"About ¥5tn a year unfunded; the fiscal input to the super-long end."},{"date":"2026-12-15","event":"BoJ Tankan (Dec; September large manufacturers +24); machinery orders (Oct)","why":"The quarterly leading read two days before the BoJ meeting."},{"date":"2026-12-18","event":"BoJ decision (meeting 17–18 Dec; pricing 76.7% for 1.50% per MogWatch); national CPI (Nov, 08:30) the same morning","why":"The base case is a hike to 1.50%; the CPI prints hours before the statement."},{"date":"2027-01-05","event":"USTR Section 301 comments and hearing requests due (hearing from 25 Jan 2027; Japan 15% in total, alternative 20%)","why":"The window in which the 20% alternative tied to the investment commitments is argued; final action expected early 2027."}],"cb":{"label":"Tightening","note":"Held at 1.25% on 30 Oct by 8–1 with Takata dissenting for 1.50%. Tokyo November CPI (27 Nov, 08:30): core 2.9% from 2.6%, the highest since July 2025 and a third month above 2%, against a 3.0% QUICK and Reuters consensus (OANDA 2.9%); headline 2.8%; core-core 2.5% from 2.6%; energy +9.5% (electricity +9.5%, city gas +13.7%) as the subsidies ended, food ex fresh 3.1%, services 2.1%, accommodation 3.6%; core m/m +0.5%. Tamura (Kofu, 26 Nov): speech, the Bank must bring the rate to neutral soon and respond swiftly to upside price signs \"including by accelerating the pace of policy interest rate hikes\"; press conference, a hike will be discussed \"at the December or a later meeting\", there is a good chance of one within about three months of the September move, 25 bp every few months is his base case and \"a 50-basis-point rate hike may become necessary\" if upside risks persist, neutral about 2%, and the AI-led equity correction can affect corporate and household decisions. Pricing: MogWatch OIS 74.7% for 18 Dec at 00:10 JST on 27 Nov, before the CPI (implied 1.437%; January 1.481%, March 1.595%, 1.75% or higher by March 43.2%), from 76.7% on 26 Nov and 84.7% on 20 Nov; the Reuters poll 86% of economists. JGBs closed 26 Nov (QUICK/NQN via SMTB): 2-year 1.740% (+1.5 bp), 5-year 2.055% (+2.5 bp), 10-year 2.645% (+2.5 bp against a revised 2.620% for 25 Nov; high 2.660%), 20-year 3.560% (−2 bp), 30-year 3.895% (−5.5 bp), 40-year 4.115% (−6.5 bp), December futures 128.24 (unchanged): the front end sold on Tamura and the super-long end rallied after the BoJ 10- to 25-year purchase operation drew 4.43 cover (3.58 last time, the highest since June), which QUICK read as heavy investor selling into the Bank. On 27 Nov futures opened 128.23 (−1 sen) and traded to 128.35 after the CPI miss; no cash yields were reported by this refresh. Under the framework the 2-year at 1.740% still prices part of a hold while the most hawkish member talks about 50 bp; the stance is Tightening with the pace disputed, and the Tokyo core at 2.9% on energy with core-core easing gives both sides a number. The yen: USD/JPY closed 159.25 in New York on 26 Nov (Gaitame; +0.08; high 159.34, low 158.95 after Tamura), 159.14 to 159.38 in Tokyo on 27 Nov with 159.19 at noon and no reaction to the CPI; no MoF or Katayama comment was found. The balance sheet was ¥626.2tn at 31 Oct. Next: 2-year auction and Ueda in Nagoya 30 Nov, October hard data 30 Nov, GDP second estimate 8 Dec, FOMC 8–9 Dec, national CPI and the BoJ decision 18 Dec."},"cbName":"BoJ stance","conflicts":[{"a":"The leading data still say Expansion: the November flash manufacturing PMI rose to 53.7 with export orders at their second-fastest growth since January 2018, October exports were a record ¥11.77tn (+20.5%), core machinery orders +13.4% y/y, the September leading index was revised up to 116.5 and the October services PPI printed +4.2% y/y, a 36-year high.","b":"The household leg is turning: services PMI 50.5 from 51.7 with new business at a four-month low, the composite at a six-month low, Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized on consumption −0.3% q/q, consumer confidence 35.0, Tokyo energy prices +9.5% in November as the subsidies ended, and the equity drawdown is 21.0% on the 26 Nov close.","read":"Leading over coincident keeps the regime Expansion on the manufacturing leg; the services side has not crossed 50 and the hard October data are not in yet, so the conflict is open.","trigger":"Services PMI under 50 in the final (3 Dec), consumer confidence under 35 on 2 Dec, or a second negative GDP quarter on 8 Dec moves the regime to Slowdown; October industrial production, retail and labour on 30 Nov are the first hard test."},{"a":"Breadth says the fall is over: on 27 Nov 1,164 Prime names rose against 345 (75%) at the morning close with 29 of 33 sectors up and TOPIX +1.56% ahead of the Nikkei +1.17%, the first broad up-session of the drawdown, and the Nikkei VI closed 26 Nov at 31.13, under 35.","b":"The index says it is not: the 26 Nov session put on 1,240 points by noon and closed up 488 on flat breadth (749 up, 751 down) with Advantest and SoftBank Group about 393 of the points, the VI spiked to 43.22 intraday, and the 27 Nov morning level of 58,172 is 3.2% under the 60,030 first-test line with the US half-session still to come.","read":"The drawdown has been four AI names on both legs for a week and the broad market is now being bought with them; that is the best read since the fall began and it is still a morning session, not a close above the lines.","trigger":"A close above 60,030 with TOPIX above 3,895 and the Nikkei VI under 35 moves the posture to Neutral; a close under 56,436.65 is a new drawdown low."},{"a":"The Bank says the pace may need to speed up: Tamura on 26 Nov said 25 bp every few months is his base case, \"a 50-basis-point rate hike may become necessary\" if upside risks persist, the rate must reach neutral (about 2%) soon, and the Tokyo core CPI printed 2.9% on 27 Nov, a third month above 2%; the Reuters poll has 86% of economists for December.","b":"The market is pricing part of a hold: the 2-year closed 1.740% on 26 Nov, under 1.75% for a second session, MogWatch OIS has December at 74.7% and March at 1.595% (1.75% or higher by March 43.2%), Tamura himself said December \"or a later meeting\", and the Tokyo core missed the 3.0% consensus with core-core easing to 2.5%.","read":"The drift is the equity drawdown and the \"or later\", not the inflation data; the market is waiting for the governor, and the Tokyo print gave the hawks the headline and the doves the core.","trigger":"A 2-year close back above 1.85% reprices December; a market-stability reference from Ueda on 30 Nov is the hold tell; a 2-year close under 1.70% says the hold is the base case."},{"a":"The yen is off the haven path: USD/JPY closed 159.25 on 26 Nov, was 159.19 at noon on 27 Nov after a Tokyo core CPI at 2.9% and a member talking about 50 bp, and the front-end gap is about 287 bp with the Fed at 97.8% for 25 bp.","b":"The haven bid and the policy bid both exist in small size: the 158.95 low on 26 Nov came in the minutes after Tamura spoke and the 158.66 low on 25 Nov with US stocks down 3% to 4% intraday, and both were bought back within hours; 160 is 75 sen away, no MoF or Katayama comment has been found since 19 Nov, month-end fixing flows come at 01:00 JST and the November intervention total prints 30 Nov.","read":"Neither an AI-confined equity fall nor a hawkish board member nor an above-target Tokyo CPI produces a yen bid that lasts; the pair is trading the rate gap in thin holiday liquidity and the 160 line is the next event.","trigger":"A close above 160 brings the MoF; a return of the broad equity fall (TOPIX under 3,674) or a US liquidation that holds takes the pair toward 158.4 and the 155 to 156 carry-unwind zone."},{"a":"The super-long end says the stress has eased: the 30-year closed 3.895% on 26 Nov (−5.5 bp) and the 40-year 4.115% (−6.5 bp), reversing the post-auction sell-off, and the 30-year is now under the 3.90% half of the pair-off condition.","b":"The rally was the Bank, not investors: the BoJ 10- to 25-year operation drew 4.43 cover, the highest since June, which QUICK read as heavy selling into the central bank, the 25 Nov 40-year auction had cleared above consensus on 2.63 cover, the MoF shelved the 2-year floater for want of demand, dealers are asking for cuts and increases by maturity for a plan MUFG expects to grow by the low ¥10tns, and the yen is at 159.25, not under 158.5.","read":"Central-bank absorption lowers the yield without changing the fiscal signal; the warn pair (super-long yields with a weak yen) stays on because the yen half is unmet and the demand evidence points the other way.","trigger":"A 30-year close above 4.05% while equities fall is the term-premium signal and 4.30% the fiscal line; a close under 3.90% with USD/JPY under 158.5 turns the warn pair off; the 30-year auction on 8 Dec and the year-end issuance plan are the demand tests."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-11-27","impact":"The early read for the 18 Dec national CPI is above target on energy with the underlying pace cooling, which keeps December priced near three-quarters and gives the hawks the headline and the market the core-core; no market moved on it.","src":"MIC via OANDA Japan, investingLive, ActionForex and Trading Economics","tag":"Data","title":"Tokyo November CPI (MIC, 08:30): core (ex fresh food) +2.9% y/y from +2.6%, the highest since July 2025 and a third month above 2%, against a 3.0% consensus (Reuters, QUICK; OANDA 2.9%); headline +2.8% from +2.7%; ex fresh food and energy +2.5% from +2.6%; energy +9.5% from +2.2% (electricity +9.5%, +14.2% m/m; city gas +13.7%, +13.0% m/m; gasoline −1.1%) as the subsidies that still covered September usage in October ended, adding about 0.34 pt to the core change; fresh food +1.8% from +8.6%; food ex fresh +3.1% (+3.0%), eating out +4.7%, prepared food +4.8%, meat +6.4%, non-Koshihikari rice −33.8%; services +2.1% (+2.2%), accommodation +3.6% (+6.7%), rent +1.9%; childcare fees −0.21 pt to March 2027; seasonally adjusted m/m: headline +0.3%, core +0.5%, core-core +0.1%. Reaction: USD/JPY 159.27 at 08:37 after 159.26 to 159.28 in the first minute; Nikkei 57,758 at 08:37 from 57,798; JGB futures opened −1 sen and traded to 128.35.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/tokyocpi-nov-2026-review/"},{"date":"2026-11-27","impact":"The first session of the drawdown in which the index and 75% of the market rose together, led by TOPIX; the levels are still 3.2% and 2.2% short of the 60,030 and 3,895 lines, and the US half-session and month-end flows follow.","src":"OANDA Japan DZH mn_1046143; Fisco via Zaikei 879444; Reuters via Newsweek Japan; OANDA Japan mn_1046144 (yen)","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo 27 Nov morning: Nikkei 58,172.04 (+672.88, +1.17%) after opening 57,669.79 (+170.63), a brief dip under the 57,499.16 prior close, a move through 58,000 mid-morning and a close near the high; TOPIX 3,809.02 (+58.62, +1.56%); Prime 1,164 up, 345 down, 40 flat (75% advancing), 29 of 33 sectors up led by nonferrous metals, oil and coal and machinery against services, land transport and retail; Kioxia, Lasertec and HOYA (brokerage upgrade) sharply higher, Advantest, SoftBank Group, Tokyo Electron, Ibiden, TDK, Murata, Fujikura, Shin-Etsu and Fanuc up, Recruit, Fast Retailing, Sony, Konami, Terumo, Nitori and KDDI down, Daido Group sharply lower on an unchanged forecast; futures 58,250 (+900); Prime turnover ¥3.44tn on 993m shares. Drivers: FTSE +1.75%, DAX +2.08%, CAC +1.61% on 26 Nov after reports the EU tariff would be lighter than feared, US futures up with the cash market closed, KOSPI up, calm JGB yields, interim-dividend reinvestment, the Tokyo core CPI miss. USD/JPY 159.19 at noon (159.14 to 159.38).","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/mn_1046143_202611271157/"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"The most hawkish member put 50 bp on the table and December \"or later\" in the same afternoon; the 2-year rose 1.5 bp to 1.740%, the yen dipped to 158.95 and recovered, and OIS slipped to 74.7% for December, so the market took the pace talk as a view and the \"or later\" as the signal.","src":"Reuters via MarketScreener; Jiji via nippon.com (EN and JP); Kyodo via OANDA Japan; ANN via ABEMA","tag":"Policy","title":"Tamura (Kofu, 26 Nov). Speech (10:00): the BoJ \"needs to bring its policy rate to a level deemed neutral to the economy soon\" because financial conditions remain accommodative; with underlying inflation less firmly anchored at 2% than in the US or Europe the Bank \"must respond swiftly to signs of an upward deviation in prices, including by accelerating the pace of policy interest rate hikes\"; \"not to go slowly, but unhurried haste\" (悠々として急ぐ); upside risks from Middle East raw-material costs, import prices on the weak yen and AI demand. Press conference (14:00, Reuters via MarketScreener, Jiji via nippon.com): a hike will be discussed \"at the December or a later meeting\" and there is a good chance of one within about three months of the September move; \"there's still about three weeks until our December policy meeting, so I would like to scrutinise various data\"; 25 bp \"once every few months\" is the base case and \"if upside price risks still exist, a 50-basis-point rate hike may become necessary\"; neutral about 2%; the AI-led stock correction could affect corporate and household decisions and the real economy; the effects of each hike can be assessed within a few months, which \"does not mean slowly\".","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/boj-s-tamura-signals-chance-of-faster-pace-interest-rate-hikes-ce7851d8dd81ff25"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"A 1,240-point AI rebound that closed as 488 on flat breadth, with the VI collapsing from 43 to 31 by the close: a two-way market in four names with the broad market unchanged, 4.2% under the first-test line.","src":"Kabutan n202611260984; Fisco via Zaikei 879302 and 879295; Gentosha Gold Online; OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045983; Fisco via Investing.com 1771495 and 1771569; Nikkei headline (night futures)","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close 26 Nov: Nikkei 57,499.16 (+487.54, +0.86%), open 57,429.54, high 58,251.93 (up more than 1,240 before noon), low 57,288.10 in the afternoon; TOPIX 3,750.40 (+4.20, +0.11%); Prime 749 up, 751 down, 17 of 33 sectors up (nonferrous metals, oil and coal, information and communication, glass, electrical equipment) against air transport, food, pharmaceuticals, insurance and construction; turnover ¥6,828.7bn on 2,242.27m shares. Contributors (15:30): Advantest 24,830 (+1,085, +234.09 points), SoftBank Group 2,894 (+197.5, +7.3%, +159.79), Kioxia 7,762 (+867, +12.57%, +61.38), TDK 2,209 (+33.88), Tokyo Electron 7,871 (+46, +23.26), Recruit +22.75, Ibiden 4,668 (+21.04), Nitto Denko +17.36, Lasertec 30,550 (+16.52), Murata +15.37, Disco +10.85; Fast Retailing 85,500 (−670, −54.21), Daikin 23,860 (−17.87), Chugai −12.14, Toyota Tsusho −11.93, Otsuka −11.12, Sony 4,448 (−10.96), HOYA −6.83; Nikkei constituents 110 up, 113 down. Nikkei VI 31.13 (−2.26, −6.77%) after 43.22 intraday (42.03 at 14:15); December futures 57,350 (+310), night session 57,890 (+540, Nikkei headline); Growth 250 652.60 (+12.00); Meiko +13.95%, MediaDo +13.82% on a dividend increase, Taisei −3.87%. Fade: a report that Nvidia asked manufacturing partners to halt server shipments to four Southeast Asian countries, the December Huang testimony, no appetite to chase before Thanksgiving, defensives sold, Tamura.","url":"https://s.kabutan.jp/news/n202611260984/"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"The front end sold on Tamura while the super-long end rallied into the Bank's bid: a twist that leaves the 2-year under 1.75% and the fiscal gauge unchanged, since the 30-year fell on central-bank absorption rather than demand.","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB; Fisco via Zaikei 879296; QUICK Money World headline; Reuters via MarketScreener and FixedIncomeNews (floating-rate bond)","tag":"Markets","title":"JGB closes 26 Nov (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, 15:27): 2-year 1.740% (+1.5 bp; Fisco 1.726%), 5-year 2.055% (+2.5 bp), 10-year 2.645% (+2.5 bp against a revised 2.620% for 25 Nov, high 2.660%; SMTB table 2.635%, Fisco 2.615%), 20-year 3.560% (−2 bp), 30-year 3.895% (−5.5 bp), 40-year 4.115% (−6.5 bp); December futures 128.24 (unchanged), range 127.96 to 128.41; TONA December futures 98.5625 (−0.0100). The BoJ purchase operation drew 4.43 cover in the 10- to 25-year bucket (3.58 last time, the highest since June), 2.51 in 3- to 5-year and 4.59 in linkers, read by a domestic strategist as weak on heavy investor selling. The MoF primary-dealer meeting on the FY2027 plan heard requests for increases and decreases by maturity (QUICK Money World headline; no readout in the pages opened); the debut 2-year floating-rate bond planned for January is postponed pending demand (Reuters, 17 Nov), with the plan due at year-end and a June review. 27 Nov open: futures 128.23 (−1 sen), high 128.35 after the Tokyo CPI; TONA 98.5700 (+0.0075); European yields rose on 26 Nov; the ¥500bn liquidity-supply auction (5 to 11 years) and the investor meeting follow.","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"A formal protest with no retaliation and a restatement that 15% is the deal: the export leg is unchanged until a rate takes effect in 2027, and the leading-index revision is a touch firmer.","src":"Novaist (Kihara); Sustainable Japan (USTR terms); Myforex DZH dzh261126130 (index revisions); OANDA Japan mn_1045961 (yen)","tag":"Trade","title":"Section 301 reaction and data, 26 Nov: Chief Cabinet Secretary Kihara (morning press conference) called Japan's inclusion in the USTR proposal regrettable (遺憾) and said this had been conveyed to the US side, that Japan has no structural overcapacity and no non-market policies or practices, that Japan and the US have cooperated on third-country overcapacity, that the US has agreed not to impose tariffs beyond the 2025 agreement, which the proposal reflects (15% in total net of MFN and the existing 12.5% duty, Section 232 goods exempt, 20% alternative if commitments are judged insufficient; comments to 5 Jan, hearing 25 Jan 2027), and that Japan will steadily implement the deal including the US investment. Cabinet Office (14:00): the September leading index was revised to 116.5 from the 116.4 preliminary and the coincident index held at 119.5. Bank of Korea decided on rates the same day (expected 3.25% from 3.00%, Fisco preview; result not in the pages opened). USD/JPY 159.16 at 15:00 Tokyo (158.95 to 159.26).","url":"https://novaist.jp/articles/japan-protests-us-tariff-plan/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"For Japan the headline rate matches the July 2025 deal and autos are exempt, so the export leg is unchanged until a rate takes effect in 2027; the 20% alternative tied to the $550bn investment commitments is the new political risk. It pushed US futures down overnight before PCE.","src":"Wiley Rein alert; Baker McKenzie Global Import Blog; Reuters via Korea Times; BigGo Finance; coki.jp","tag":"Trade","title":"USTR (25 Nov, US time) issued affirmative Section 301 determinations on structural excess capacity and proposed tariffs on 14 economies: Japan 15% in total net of MFN and the existing 12.5% July 2026 tariff, with an alternative of up to 20% if consultations on the deal commitments are judged unsatisfactory; the EU 15% (alternative 20%), Korea and Taiwan 15% (25%), Switzerland 15% (20%), China up to 20% with Annex A goods 10% and Annex B exempt (up to 40% if engagement fails), Vietnam up to 35% (20% with an agreement), India up to 25% (17%), Singapore up to 20% (10%), Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand and Cambodia up to 18% (25%), Bangladesh up to 18% (20%); Mexico and Norway still under investigation. For the 13 non-China economies goods under Section 232 (autos and parts, steel, aluminium, copper, semiconductors, patented pharmaceuticals, wood) are exempt and the new rate is reduced by the forced-labour 301 duties, so the duties do not stack. Comments and hearing requests due 5 Jan 2027; hearing from 25 Jan 2027 at the ITC; final action expected early 2027. Greer: the caps match the deals already reached. The report cites Japan's industrial plans, BoJ-era financial repression, NISA, zombie firms, the technical-intern system and Rapidus and JASM subsidies (coki). No Japanese government reaction was found.","url":"https://www.wiley.law/alert-USTR-Announces-Affirmative-Section-301-Determinations-and-Proposes-New-Tariffs-on-14-Trading-Partners-to-Address-Structural-Excess-Capacity"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"A late recovery after a morning liquidation in AI names, hot core PCE and the Section 301 proposal: yields fell anyway, which is the haven trade, and the Fed hike is fully priced. Tokyo took the recovery, not the fall, on 26 Nov.","src":"CappNotes closing look; Motley Fool; GSM Global; OANDA Japan NY digest mn_1045891; Fisco via Zaikei 879162 (US data)","tag":"Markets","title":"Wall Street 25 Nov: S&P 500 7,182.78 (−18.31, −0.25%) after −3.2% intraday, Nasdaq 23,585.80 (−147.74, −0.62%) after −4.6%, Dow 50,182.95 (+164.25, +0.33%) after a fall of more than 1,100, Russell 2000 2,704.06 (+0.71%), VIX 28.29 (+7.3%) after topping 30; Nvidia $149.30 (−$6.08, −3.91%, low $141.17), AMD $406.03 (−1.75%), Micron −4.53%, Broadcom $300.86 (−0.07%), Anthropic $82.79 (+1.07%, under the $83 IPO), HP +11.49%; SOXX −0.50%, SMH −1.01% (SOX close not found). Data: October PCE +0.3% m/m and 3.8% y/y, core PCE +0.3% m/m and 3.3% y/y (consensus 3.2%, September 3.1%); Q3 GDP second estimate 4.3% annualized (consensus 3.5%, advance 3.4%) with consumption 4.2%; durable goods +0.2% (consensus +0.7%), ex-transport +0.5%; initial claims 189,000 (200,000); new home sales 701,000 (650,000); personal income +0.7%. US 2-year 4.61% (−2 bp), 10-year 4.85% (−6 to −8 bp; 4.79% low), 30-year 5.24%; CME FedWatch 97.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec (Gaitame 96.4%; Polymarket 94%, Kalshi 93%); dollar index 103.71 (−0.10%); WTI $83.68 (+1.99%), Brent $94.61 (+3.51%) after a Houthi strike on Ras Tanura; gold $3,534.70 (−0.71%), under $3,500 intraday for the first time since August 2025. US markets closed 26 Nov, early close 27 Nov.","url":"https://www.fool.com/coverage/stock-market-today/2026/11/25/stock-market-today-nov-25-nvidia-slides-3-9-as-the-market-considers-hot-pce-inflation-data-and-new-tariff-proposals/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"Bull-flattening to 20 years on the haven bid and a hold being priced at the front end, while the super-long end cheapened on supply: the two halves of the curve are now sending opposite signals and the 40-year at 4.18% is the fiscal gauge into the MoF meetings.","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB; Fisco via Zaikei 879100; stock-marketdata.com; Newsquawk","tag":"Markets","title":"JGB closes 25 Nov (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, 15:36): 2-year 1.720% (−5.5 bp; Fisco 1.722%), 5-year 2.030% (−8 bp), 10-year 2.645% (−7.5 bp, lowest in about three weeks; Fisco 2.627%, stock-marketdata.com 2.620%), 20-year 3.580% (−4.5 bp; Fisco 3.556%), 30-year 3.950% (+1.5 bp), new 40-year 4.180% (+6 bp); December futures 128.24 (+64 sen), open 127.66, high 128.39 (highest since 6 Nov). The ¥300bn 40-year reopening (19th issue, 3.8% coupon, results 12:35) cleared at a 4.09% highest accepted yield, above the 4.070% to 4.080% consensus, on 2.63 cover against 3.10 in September, called somewhat weak by a domestic strategist on investor demand; the new 40-year traded 4.145% before the sale. Drivers: the Nikkei down more than 2,000 intraday, AI selling on Nasdaq on 24 Nov, US 10-year under 4.90% in Tokyo hours. MoF primary-dealer meeting 26 Nov, investor meeting 27 Nov.","url":"https://www.zaikei.co.jp/article/20261125/879100.html"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The index fell 2.78% while 64% of the market rose: the drawdown is confined to the AI complex and the close held 575 points above the 20 Nov liquidation low. The posture stays Risk-off on the index, with breadth the best since the fall began.","src":"TBL Advisory 20261125nk; Tokyo Hodo Shimbun 30906; OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045754; QUICK Money World 223679; Fisco via Zaikei 879109 (VI); Kabutan headline (night session)","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close 25 Nov: Nikkei 57,011.62 (−1,633.01, −2.78%), open 58,383.49, low 56,502.97 (down more than 2,100 in the morning; morning close 56,710.43), Prime turnover ¥7.4977tn on 2,734m shares, Prime 993 up, 537 down, 22 flat (63.9% advancing), 23 of 33 sectors up: pharmaceuticals +5.48%, real estate +3.54%, food +3.53%, retail +2.95% against nonferrous metals −8.69%, electrical equipment −4.48%, glass and ceramics −3.79%. Advantest about −762 index points and Tokyo Electron −567 (together about 1,328), SoftBank Group −379 points (−14.8%, year-to-date low), Ibiden −115, Fujikura 2,062.5 (−14.7%, downgraded), Murata 4,633 (−14.2%); Fast Retailing 86,170 (+2,130, +172 points), Chugai 7,501 (+426, +43), Terumo up, Ono Pharmaceutical 2,699 (+10.7%) on an Amgen patent settlement. Nikkei VI 33.39 (+1.88, +5.97%; high 43.23). December futures 57,040 (−1,940); night session opened 56,660 and closed 57,370 (Kabutan headline). TOPIX 3,746.20 (−18.68, −0.50%), Growth 250 640.60 (−14.24). USD/JPY 159.21 to 159.23 at 17:00.","url":"https://tbladvisory.com/20261125nk/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"Services prices are running well above 2% at the corporate level, which supports the BoJ's December case even as the market trims it to about 78%.","src":"Bank of Japan","tag":"Data","title":"October services PPI (BoJ, 08:50): +4.2% y/y from a revised 3.8%, the fastest since March 1990, m/m +1.1%; ex-international transport +3.2% (3.0%); international transport +99.1% (ocean freight +120.4%), transport and postal +9.4%, accommodation +12.1%, leasing and rental +11.8%, real estate +3.0%, information and communications +2.5%, advertising −1.2%.","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/statistics/pi/cspi_release/sppi2610.pdf"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Monday's Huang rally unwound in the chips and memory names that lead the Nikkei while the Dow rose; the rotation out of AI is the Tokyo driver on 25 Nov.","src":"Company Chronicle; Motley Fool; TradingKey; OANDA Japan NY digest","tag":"Markets","title":"Wall Street 24 Nov: S&P 500 7,201.09 (−36.08, −0.50%), Nasdaq 23,733.54 (−356.02, −1.48%), Nasdaq 100 −1.99%, Dow 50,018.70 (+423.39, +0.85%), Russell 2000 2,684.94 (+0.57%), VIX 26.36; Philadelphia Semiconductor Index −7.88%; Nvidia $155.38 (−$9.67, −5.86%, more than −8% intraday, 379m shares; Bernstein upgrade to Outperform, $200), AMD $413.27 (−8.86%), Micron −10.97%, SanDisk −11.13%, HPE −16.31%, Broadcom $301.08 (−3.98%), Anthropic $81.91 (−6.5%, under the $83 IPO price, $75.61 low), Microsoft +0.7%, Apple +1.4%; Dell: AI orders $60.2bn, backlog $132.3bn, inventory nearly tripled; US 10-year 4.90% to 4.93%; gold $3,561.9 (−89.5); WTI $82.05 (+1.88).","url":"https://www.fool.com/coverage/stock-market-today/2026/11/24/stock-market-today-nov-24-nvidia-gives-back-usd240-billion-in-market-value-despite-wall-street-upgrade-signaling-29-upside/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"The yen weakened on the day the Nikkei rose and stayed weak as it fell, with the front-end gap about 290 bp; December BoJ pricing is drifting lower without a BoJ word.","src":"OANDA Japan NY digest mn_1045662; Fisco via Zaikei 878985; OANDA Japan FOMC preview; MogWatch","tag":"Policy","title":"US rates and the yen, 24 Nov: USD/JPY closed 159.26 in New York (OANDA, +0.24; Fisco 159.35) after a 159.58 high and 158.99 low; EUR/JPY 174.64 (+1.27), EUR/USD 1.0966 (+0.0063), dollar index low 103.75; US 10-year 4.93% (−1 bp; Company Chronicle 4.90%); November consumer confidence 103.3 (consensus 92.4, October 91.9), Richmond Fed 3 (−2), Case-Shiller +2.6% y/y, FHFA 0.0% m/m; CME FedWatch 95.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec, 4.2% for 50 bp, cumulative 50 bp by January 65.8% (OANDA, 24 Nov evening). BoJ OIS: MogWatch 78.2% for 18 Dec at 01:21 JST on 25 Nov (implied 1.445%), OANDA 78.7%.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/20261124_fomc_dec_preview/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"The first post-holiday close confirmed a 3.91% rebound that still failed the 60,030 and 3,895 lines, while bonds rallied with crude lower.","src":"News On Japan; ad-hoc-news/AP; Reuters via WHTC; Kyodo via OANDA Japan; Fisco via Zaikei 878940; Trading Economics","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close 24 Nov: Nikkei 58,644.63 (+2,207.98, +3.91%), high 59,262.57, up more than 2,800 at one point (Jiji), Prime turnover about ¥8.07tn, 26 of 33 sectors up led by nonferrous metals, electrical equipment and glass; Advantest +12.5%, Tokyo Electron +7.7% (AP), Ibiden limit-up, Murata and Taiyo Yuden sharply higher, Baycurrent a record; Inpex, Kobe Bussan, Nippon Shinyaku, Dentsu and KDDI fell. TOPIX 3,764.88 (+90.66, +2.47%), ending five straight falls. JGBs: 2-year 1.765% (−5.5 bp), 5-year 2.12%, 10-year 2.720% (−6.5 bp, 2.695% intraday), 20-year 3.63%, 30-year 3.94%, 40-year 4.12%, December futures 127.60 (+60 sen, 127.15 to 127.84). Reuters poll (22 analysts, 16 to 24 Nov): Nikkei 65,000 by June 2027, 67,500 by end-2027, 69,000 by mid-2028.","url":"https://newsonjapan.com/article/151515.php"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Manufacturing keeps the regime in Expansion and the services slide is the first survey evidence of the equity fall reaching households; the composite at a six-month low with confidence at a 2020 low is the warn for the household leg.","src":"Reuters via Investing.com; ActionForex; au.investing; IndexBox","tag":"Data","title":"November flash PMIs (S&P Global / au Jibun Bank, 08:30): manufacturing 53.7 from 53.3 (consensus 53.5), output 53.8 from 53.0, new orders solid, manufacturing export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018 on Asian semiconductor and electronics demand, employment up, outstanding work rising a twelfth month; services 50.5 from 51.7 with new business at a four-month low; composite 51.5 from 52.1, a six-month low; business confidence joint-lowest since August 2020; input costs an eight-month low, output charges the weakest since March. Fiddes: 'momentum was lost amid a slowdown in service sector activity'.","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661847-japan-pmi-growth-slows-to-six-month-low-as-services-weaken-despite-manufacturing-strength/"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The governance shock that drove the 20 Nov liquidation reversed in one session; the SEC and DOJ inquiries continue (Al Jazeera), so the AI chain's governance strand is open even as the price has recovered.","src":"Nvidia newsroom and SEC 8-K; Al Jazeera; Motley Fool; Fox Business; STL.News; TBL Advisory","tag":"Markets","title":"Nvidia's board 'voted unanimously' to reinstate Jensen Huang as president, CEO and director effective immediately, citing 'input from senior levels of the United States government', 'no national security risk' and satisfaction with his 'candor', with an independent-director investigation of the reported foreign-intelligence effort, a new National Security and Export Control Committee, a chief compliance officer search and continued cooperation with government investigations; Kress continues as CFO. Trump: 'the correct and proper decision', he was 'represented in the board meeting'. Nvidia $165.05 (+$39.52, +31.48%, range $156.93 to $171.50, 772.5m shares, about $3.03tn; largest one-day percentage gain in more than two decades per Motley Fool); S&P 500 7,237.17 (+326.78, +4.73%), Nasdaq 24,089.56 (+1,471.82, +6.51%), Dow 49,595.31 (+1,447.98, +3.01%), Russell 2000 2,669.66 (+3.40%), VIX 26.62 from 35.89; Micron +19%, AMD $453.46 (+6.85%), Broadcom $313.55 (+3.00%), Oracle +12.34%, Tesla +11.5%, Meta +6.6%, Anthropic $87.64 (−1.4%). Chicago Nikkei futures 57,960.","url":"https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-board-reinstates-jensen-huang-as-president-ceo-and-board-director"}],"headline":"Tokyo November core CPI rose to 2.9% from 2.6%, the highest since July 2025, under the 3.0% consensus, on energy +9.5% after the subsidies ended while core-core eased to 2.5%; USD/JPY did not move (159.19 at noon). Tamura said a 50 bp hike \"may become necessary\" if upside risks persist, with 25 bp every few months his base case and December \"or later\" on the table; MogWatch OIS has December at 74.7%. Tokyo closed 26 Nov at 57,499.16 (+0.86%) on Advantest, SoftBank Group and Kioxia after a 1,240-point morning gain faded, TOPIX 3,750.40 (+0.11%), Nikkei VI 31.13; JGBs sold at the front end (2-year 1.740%, +1.5 bp) and rallied at the super-long end (30-year 3.895%, −5.5 bp; 40-year 4.115%) after a BoJ operation drew 4.43 cover; the Nikkei was 58,172.04 (+1.17%) at the 27 Nov morning close with 75% of Prime up.","marketClose":"Thu 26 Nov 2026","notes":["This refresh ran at about 05:00 UTC (14:00 JST) on Friday 27 Nov, during the Tokyo afternoon session, so the Nikkei, TOPIX, JGB and USD/JPY rows and the history hold the 26 Nov closes (marketClose); the 27 Nov morning-session levels and the Tokyo CPI are in the text only. The 27 Nov cash JGB yields, the ¥500bn liquidity-supply auction result, the MoF investor-meeting readout and the Bank of Korea decision were not available in any page opened.","Nikkei 26 Nov close 57,499.16 (+487.54, +0.86%), open 57,429.54, high 58,251.93, low 57,288.10, breadth 749 / 751, 17 sectors up, volume 2,242.27m shares and Growth 250 are Kabutan; contributors are Fisco via Zaikei 879302 (15:30 snapshot; percentage moves derived from price and change); turnover ¥6,828.7bn is Gentosha Gold Online; TOPIX 3,750.40 (+4.20) is Kabutan, Fisco and OANDA Japan, which agree; December futures 57,350 (+310) is OANDA Japan (contract month not stated); the night-session 57,890 (+540) is a Nikkei headline only. Nikkei VI 31.13 (−2.26) is Fisco via Investing.com at 16:35, and the 43.22 intraday high is from the same item (the 14:15 item gave 42.43 as the high to that point).","JGBs 26 Nov are QUICK/NQN 15:27 closes via SMTB (2-year 1.740%, 5-year 2.055%, 10-year 2.645%, 20-year 3.560%, 30-year 3.895%, 40-year 4.115%, futures 128.24). QUICK's own 26 Nov changes imply 25 Nov closes of 2.620% for the 10-year and 1.725% for the 2-year, against the 2.645% and 1.720% its 25 Nov 15:36 item reported and the previous refresh recorded; the 2-year and 10-year rows carry the revised QUICK basis as the prior and the changes are QUICK's. The SMTB market table shows the 10-year at 2.635% (−1.0 bp) and Fisco at 2.615%, so the 10-year sources span 3 bp. MoF reference rates for 25 Nov (jp-bond.com, compound): 2-year 1.731%, 10-year 2.713%, 30-year 3.998%. The BoJ operation cover ratios and the \"weak\" comment are QUICK/NQN.","Tokyo CPI: the consensus for core is 3.0% per Reuters (investingLive), QUICK (via SMTB) and Trading Economics and 2.9% per OANDA Japan, so the print is recorded as a miss against 3.0%; the October headline is given as 2.7% by OANDA and ActionForex while the previous refresh recorded 2.8%, and the November headline of 2.8% is used as published. Components are OANDA Japan and ActionForex. USD/JPY 159.25 for 26 Nov is Gaitame's New York close (Myforex DZH gives 159.27 at 04:00 JST and a 158.95 to 159.34 range); no OANDA New York digest for 26 Nov was found. Oil and gold for 26 Nov are intraday levels at 08:30 GMT (Edge) and were not used in the rows.","Tamura: the speech quotes are Reuters via Investing.com and AOL; the press-conference quotes (50 bp, 25 bp every few months, December or later, three weeks of data, neutral about 2%) are Reuters via MarketScreener, with the second half of that page summarised rather than verbatim, and Jiji via nippon.com (English and Japanese). The Bloomberg write-up (financial conditions judged within three months) was not fetched. MogWatch 74.7% is as of 00:10 JST on 27 Nov, before the Tokyo CPI; no post-CPI OIS reading was found (FXStreet headlines of 70% and 80% were not opened). The MoF primary-dealer readout is a QUICK Money World headline only; the floating-rate bond postponement is Reuters of 17 Nov, before the meeting. Calendar and next-release dates are Tokyo dates; the 30-year auction date (8 Dec) is MUFG's weekly."],"posture":{"label":"Risk-off","note":"Risk-off. Tokyo closed 26 Nov at 57,499.16 (+487.54, +0.86%) after opening 57,429.54, rising more than 1,240 to 58,251.93 before noon and fading to a 57,288.10 low in the afternoon; TOPIX 3,750.40 (+4.20, +0.11%). The gain was again the AI names: Advantest 24,830 (+1,085, +234 index points), SoftBank Group 2,894 (+7.3%, +160), Kioxia 7,762 (+12.6%, +61), TDK +34, Tokyo Electron +23; Fast Retailing 85,500 (−670, −54 points), Daikin −18; Prime breadth was flat at 749 up against 751 down with 17 of 33 sectors up (nonferrous metals, oil, information and communication, glass, electrical equipment) against air transport, food, pharmaceuticals, insurance and construction; turnover ¥6.83tn on 2,242m shares; December futures 57,350 (+310), night session 57,890 (+540, Nikkei headline); Nikkei VI closed 31.13 (−2.26) after a 43.22 intraday high. Gains faded on a report that Nvidia asked partners to halt server shipments to four Southeast Asian countries and on Tamura. Tokyo 27 Nov morning: Nikkei 58,172.04 (+672.88, +1.17%) after a 57,669.79 open and a brief dip under the prior close; TOPIX 3,809.02 (+58.62, +1.56%); Prime 1,164 up against 345 down (75%), 29 of 33 sectors up; Kioxia, Lasertec and HOYA sharply higher, Recruit and Fast Retailing lower; futures 58,250 (+900); turnover ¥3.44tn; European stocks rose 1.6% to 2.1% on 26 Nov and US futures rose with the cash market closed. Under the framework the drawdown is 21.0% from the 72,831.73 record on the 26 Nov close, the 20 Nov liquidation close (56,436.65) is untested, breadth on 27 Nov is the broadest since the fall began, and the Nikkei VI is back under 35. The posture stays Risk-off until a close retakes 60,030 and 3,895 with the VI under 35; the morning levels are 3.2% and 2.2% short of those lines."},"regime":{"label":"Expansion","note":"One new leading print: the September leading index was revised to 116.5 from the 116.4 preliminary and the coincident index held at 119.5 (Cabinet Office, 26 Nov). The November flash PMIs stand: manufacturing 53.7 from 53.3, output 53.8, export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018; services 50.5 from 51.7, composite 51.5, a six-month low. The leading set is in expansion on the manufacturing leg: BoJ Tankan +24, Reuters Tankan +27 and +35, September core machinery orders +13.4% y/y with a +4.3% q/q Q4 plan, consumer confidence 35.0, housing starts −2.7%. Coincident: October exports +20.5% y/y to a record ¥11.77tn, Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized on consumption −0.3% q/q, industrial production +1.7%, real wages +2.2%, unemployment 2.4%, jobs ratio 1.19, retail +2.3%. Prices: October national core CPI 2.2%, at target; November Tokyo core 2.9% (energy +9.5% after the subsidies ended) with core-core easing to 2.5%, so the household squeeze is energy, not demand. The USTR Section 301 proposal (Japan 15% in total, autos exempt, 20% alternative) drew a formal protest from Kihara on 26 Nov and takes effect no earlier than 2027, so the export leg is unchanged. The framework reads leading over coincident; manufacturing is at the top of its five-year range and the regime stays Expansion with the household side the weak leg and the services PMI 0.5 point from the warn band. A services PMI under 50 in the final (3 Dec) or the December flash, consumer confidence under 35 on 2 Dec, or a second negative GDP quarter on 8 Dec moves it to Slowdown; October industrial production, retail and labour print 30 Nov."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"+0.4","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":50},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":53},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":55.1},{"l":"May 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":54.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":54.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":54.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":53.3},{"l":"Nov 26","v":53.7}],"latest":"53.7","name":"au Jibun Bank Manufacturing PMI","next":"early Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 (flash)","prior":"53.3","read":"The November flash rose to 53.7 from 53.3 in October (consensus 53.5), the first rise since the 54.9 of August. Output 53.8 from 53.0, new orders grew at a solid pace and export orders had their second-fastest growth since January 2018 on semiconductor and electronics demand from other Asian economies; employment rose, led by manufacturing, and outstanding work grew for a twelfth month. Input cost inflation slowed to an eight-month low and output charges to the weakest since March. Business confidence across both sectors fell to its joint-lowest since August 2020, so the order books are strong and the mood is not. Well above 50 and at the top of the five-year range: good. The final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion, chip-led","src":"S&P Global flash via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex, au.investing and IndexBox (24 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Up; beat 53.5","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661847-japan-pmi-growth-slows-to-six-month-low-as-services-weaken-despite-manufacturing-strength/"},{"change":"−1.2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":51.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":53.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":53.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":51},{"l":"May 26","v":50},{"l":"Jun 26","v":52.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":51.3},{"l":"Oct 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Nov 26","v":50.5}],"latest":"50.5","name":"au Jibun Bank Services PMI","next":"early Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 (flash)","prior":"51.7","read":"The November flash fell to 50.5 from 51.7, the lowest since the 50.0 of May, with new business growth at a four-month low; the composite fell to 51.5 from 52.1, a six-month low, and activity still expanded for a twentieth month. Business confidence is at its joint-lowest since August 2020, with manufacturers more optimistic than services firms; S&P Global's Fiddes said 'momentum was lost amid a slowdown in service sector activity' and that 'uncertainty remains elevated'. This is the first survey taken through the equity fall and the first sign of it in the household-facing data. Still above 50 so the signal stays good by the 50 line, but it is 0.5 point from the warn band and the divergence with manufacturing (53.7) is the widest since the spring; the final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Just above 50; composite six-month low","src":"S&P Global flash via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex and au.investing (24 Nov)","trend":"down","trendWord":"New business four-month low","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economic-indicators/japans-factory-activity-expands-in-november-pmi-shows-5046775"},{"change":"+2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 23","v":12},{"l":"Mar 24","v":11},{"l":"Jun 24","v":13},{"l":"Sep 24","v":13},{"l":"Dec 24","v":14},{"l":"Mar 25","v":12},{"l":"Jun 25","v":13},{"l":"Sep 25","v":14},{"l":"Dec 25","v":15},{"l":"Mar 26","v":17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":22},{"l":"Sep 26","v":24}],"latest":"+24","name":"Tankan Large Manufacturers DI","next":"mid-Dec (Dec survey)","period":"Sep 2026 survey","prior":"+22","read":"Released 1 Oct. Large manufacturers improved for a sixth quarter to +24, the best since March 2018, while large non-manufacturers slipped to +35 from +37. The December outlook DI is +21 for manufacturers and +30 for non-manufacturers, so firms expect some cooling. FY2026 capex plans for large firms are +11.3%. The monthly Reuters Tankan (11 Nov, survey 27 Oct to 6 Nov) points the other way for December: manufacturers +27 from +22 (consensus +23), the highest since August 2021, non-manufacturers +35 from +23, the highest since June 2018, with electronics +39 on memory-chip demand and the February outlook +28 and +29; respondents cited rising prices and interest rates as the drag. The December BoJ Tankan is due mid-December.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = favorable equals unfavorable","signal":"good","signalWord":"Highest since 2018","src":"Bank of Japan; Reuters Tankan via investingLive and RTTNews","trend":"up","trendWord":"Improving","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/statistics/tk/yoshi/tk2609.htm"},{"change":"−0.4","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":37.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":37.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":37.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":40},{"l":"Mar 26","v":33.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":32.2},{"l":"May 26","v":33.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":33.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":34.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":35.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":35.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":35}],"latest":"35.0","name":"Consumer Confidence Index","next":"2 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"35.4","read":"October fell to 35.0 from 35.4, a touch under the 35.1 consensus and the lowest since July, with every component lower: livelihood 33.2 (33.8), willingness to buy durables 26.8 (27.3), income growth 40.4 (40.5), employment 39.4 (40.0). The index has been in the 32 to 36 band since the March drop from 40.0 when the Hormuz closure lifted fuel prices. The October Eco Watchers survey (10 Nov) was also soft: the current index fell to 46.1 from 47.0 (consensus 46.8), households 45.5 (−0.9), corporate 47.6 (+0.5), and the outlook index fell to 46.5 from 47.4. Energy prices turned positive again in the Tokyo CPI in October, which explains the slip; the November reading prints on 2 Dec.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = neutral line","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 40 band","src":"Cabinet Office via Trading Economics; Eco Watchers via RTTNews, FX.co and Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lowest since July","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"−5.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":12.5},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-6.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":16.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":13.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":24.7},{"l":"Mar 26","v":5.9},{"l":"Apr 26","v":15.6},{"l":"May 26","v":-1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":16.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":11.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":18.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":13.4}],"latest":"+13.4%","name":"Core Machinery Orders, y/y","next":"mid-Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+18.6%","read":"Released 19 Nov. Core orders (private, ex ships and electric power) fell 0.3% on the month to ¥1,066.2bn after +5.2% in August (Newsquawk consensus −0.5%, RTTNews +1.3%), the first fall in two months, and rose 13.4% from a year earlier against +13.7% expected and +18.6% in August. Manufacturers' orders rose 3.9% m/m to ¥547.6bn and non-manufacturers' ex utilities 1.1% to ¥547.2bn; government orders fell 1.3%, overseas orders rose 6.2%, total orders were flat. The Cabinet Office kept its 'signs of a pickup' assessment and the Q4 (Oct to Dec) forecast is +4.3% q/q for core orders, +2.6% for manufacturers, +5.2% for non-manufacturers and +8.8% for total orders. The series is noisy; the Tankan capex plans (+11.3% for large firms) and October machine tool orders (+42.5% y/y, the sixteenth straight gain) are the cleaner reads and point the same way. A double-digit y/y gain with a positive Q4 plan keeps the capex lead in place; the heat has come off the August beat.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Small miss; Q4 plan +4.3% q/q","src":"Cabinet Office via FX.co, RTTNews, Newsquawk, Kyodo (OANDA Japan) and Japan Planners (Q4 forecast)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slower, still double-digit","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3299479"},{"change":"−8.8 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":3.2},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-8.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-1.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-0.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-4.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-29.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":11.4},{"l":"May 26","v":33.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":18.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":8.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-2.7}],"latest":"−2.7%","name":"Housing Starts, y/y","next":"late Nov (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+6.1%","read":"Housing starts fell 2.7% y/y in September against a +2.9% consensus, the first fall since March after five positive months (+33.9% in May to +6.1% in August). Built-for-sale −4.5%, owned −3.4%, rented −1.6%, issued +18.4%. The comparison month, September 2025, was itself −7.3%, so the level is weak rather than flattered by the base. With the 10-year JGB near 2.8% and mortgage rates rising after three BoJ hikes, the housing leg of the leading set has turned down.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Falls on a weak base, missed","src":"MLIT via RTTNews and Newsquawk","trend":"down","trendWord":"Turns negative","url":"https://www.rttnews.com/3706092/japan-housing-starts-fall-2-7-in-september.aspx"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"−1.0 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2},{"l":"May 26","v":-2.1},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.7}],"latest":"+1.7%","name":"Industrial Production, y/y","next":"30 Nov (Oct prelim)","period":"Sep 2026 (final)","prior":"+2.7%","read":"The September final (METI, 16 Nov) revised output to +2.0% m/m from the +2.2% preliminary and to +1.7% y/y from +1.9% (consensus +1.9%), after −2.4% m/m in August; a ninth positive y/y month, slower on the base. The preliminary detail stands: eight of fifteen sectors rose, led by motor vehicles, production machinery and general-purpose and business-oriented machinery, with iron and steel down; shipments +0.6%, inventories +2.5%. METI's survey projects +5.1% in October and +0.8% in November, so Q4 starts with output catching up to the order book even as Q3 GDP fell 0.1% annualized on consumption and imports. October preliminary is due 30 Nov.","signal":"good","signalWord":"m/m rebound held at +2.0%","src":"METI final via Newsquawk and FX.co (16 Nov); preliminary via investingLive and RTTNews","trend":"down","trendWord":"Revised down","url":"https://www.newsquawk.com/headlines/japanese-industrial-production-final-sep-yy-17-prev-27"},{"change":"−0.6 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-0.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-0.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.1},{"l":"May 26","v":5.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":0.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.3}],"latest":"+2.3%","name":"Retail Sales, y/y","next":"late Nov (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+2.9%","read":"September rose 2.3% y/y against a 3.3% consensus (Trading Economics 3.8%) and fell 0.7% m/m after −1.2% in August; August was revised up to +2.9% from +2.7%. A seventh straight y/y gain, but the slowest since June and the second miss in a row. The household survey for September (10 Nov) was worse: spending fell 6.8% y/y against −3.1% and 2.8% m/m against +0.3%, the tenth straight annual fall and the steepest since September 2020, which the ministry blamed on rain every day of the month in Tokyo; the survey is small and volatile. With headline CPI at 2.1% in September, real retail growth is near zero and household spending is negative in real terms, consistent with confidence at 35.0.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Missed; household spending −6.8%","src":"METI via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slowing","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/retail-sales-annual"},{"change":"−0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.5},{"l":"May 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.4}],"latest":"2.4%","name":"Unemployment Rate","next":"late Nov to 1 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"2.5%","read":"September fell to 2.4% against a 2.5% consensus, matching July's 12-month low. Unemployed fell 100K to 1.67mn, employment rose 340K to 68.68mn, a four-month high, and the labour force rose 250K to 70.34mn. The range since 2022 is 2.4% to 2.8%, so the labour market is at the tight end of it.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Tight","src":"MIC via FX.co; history FRED","trend":"down","trendWord":"Back to the low","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3259063"},{"change":"+0.01","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.18},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1.18},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.19},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.19},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.18},{"l":"May 26","v":1.17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.19}],"latest":"1.19","name":"Jobs-to-Applicants Ratio","next":"late Nov to 1 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"1.18","read":"Rose to 1.19 from 1.18 (consensus 1.18), the first increase since June and the highest since December, but still below the 1.2 level the framework treats as tight and down from 1.35 in late 2022. With unemployment at 2.4% and employment at a four-month high, labour demand firmed in September; the Tankan employment DI (−38 all firms) says shortages remain severe.","ref":1,"refLabel":"1.0 = one opening per applicant","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 1.2 line","src":"MHLW via FX.co","trend":"up","trendWord":"First rise since Jun","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3258824"},{"change":"+3.8 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":6.1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":5.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":16.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":4.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":11.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":14.8},{"l":"May 26","v":17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":19.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":23.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":19.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":16.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":20.5}],"latest":"+20.5%","name":"Exports, y/y","next":"mid-Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"+16.7%","read":"October exports rose 20.5% y/y to ¥11,768.8bn, the highest monthly value on record and the 14th straight gain, against a +20.0% consensus and September's +16.7% (Trading Economics shows September revised to +16.5%). Export volume rose 5.8%, so the gain is not only price. Semiconductors +49.7% by value, electrical machinery +33.6%, machinery +21.2%, transport equipment +18.0% with motor vehicles +19.9%; by destination the US +23.9% (motor vehicles to the US +37.2%, surplus ¥690.6bn), Asia +22.0%, the EU +22.1%, China +13.3% (chip equipment to China +42.6%, motor vehicles to China −53.3%), the Middle East −15.0%. Imports rose 21.6% to ¥12.25tn against about +25.8% expected, on mineral fuels +36.5% and petroleum +60.5% by value for 0.7% less volume (crude from the US +738% by volume, from the Middle East −16.5%); the deficit was ¥476.5bn against ¥955bn expected, a sixth monthly deficit. External demand is the strongest leg of the economy and the chip chain is its whole engine; the US tariff deal is not showing in autos to the US (+37.2%). Next: November trade in mid-December.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Beat; chips +49.7%, volume +5.8%","src":"MoF trade statistics (provisional) via Xinhua, ActionForex, IndexBox and Trading Economics; Newsquawk (consensus); Zaikei (QUICK consensus)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Record, 14th gain","url":"https://english.news.cn/asiapacific/20261118/a9e3802d03274a27b6508b13f7aa7ff5/c.html"},{"change":"+0.6 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":-0.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-0.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.9},{"l":"May 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"+2.2%","name":"Real Wages, y/y","next":"7–8 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+1.6%","read":"September real wages rose 2.2% y/y, a ninth straight gain and above the 1.3% consensus, after August was revised to +1.6% from +1.5%. Nominal cash earnings rose 4.6% to ¥311,409, the fastest since January 1997 and above the 3.8% consensus, an eighth month above 3%; August was revised to 3.9%. Base pay rose 3.7% (3.8% in August), same-sample full-time base pay 3.1% (2.9%) and overtime 3.6%; special payments jumped 32.6%, which the ministry attributes to a statistical quirk, so the headline overstates the trend. The deflator CPI was 2.4% (2.2%). The BoJ's virtuous-cycle test is met on base pay but not yet on spending: September household spending fell 6.8% y/y (10 Nov), so the income gain is being saved or lost to weather. The Summary of Opinions has one member expecting next year's wage growth to be consistent with 2% and another expecting little change from this year. October wages print 7–8 Dec.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = wages keep pace with prices","signal":"good","signalWord":"Positive 9 months","src":"MHLW via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex and Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Faster growth","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/japans-real-wages-rise-for-ninth-month-in-september-backing-boj-rate-hike-5006105"},{"change":"−1.5 pt","hist":[{"l":"Dec 23","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 24","v":-0.4},{"l":"Jun 24","v":-0.5},{"l":"Sep 24","v":3.4},{"l":"Dec 24","v":1.6},{"l":"Mar 25","v":2},{"l":"Jun 25","v":0.5},{"l":"Sep 25","v":-1.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.1},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-0.1}],"latest":"−0.1%","name":"Real GDP, q/q annualized","next":"8 Dec (Q3 2nd prelim)","period":"Q3 2026 (1st prelim)","prior":"+1.4%","read":"Real GDP fell 0.02% q/q and 0.1% annualized in July to September (first preliminary, 16 Nov), against a +0.1% q/q and about +0.3% annualized consensus (range −0.3% to +1.5%), the first contraction in four quarters; Q2 was revised to +0.4% q/q and +1.4% annualized. Private consumption fell 0.3% q/q for a second quarter (consensus −0.2%) on food and household goods, weather hits to clothing and lodging, and payback from pre-tax-change purchases of air conditioners and cars; housing −1.1%, government consumption −0.0% (first negative in six quarters), public investment −1.1%, exports −0.2% with R&D services down, imports +2.5% (crude re-sourced after Hormuz), so net exports took 0.6 pt while domestic demand added 0.6 pt. Business investment rose 1.6% q/q (+6.8% annualized, consensus +1.5%) on AI servers and chip equipment, the first rise in three quarters. Nominal GDP +0.4% q/q and +1.6% annualized; deflator +2.4% y/y. Analysts (Meiji Yasuda, Daiwa) read the consumption weakness as price-driven and no bar to a December hike; Mizuho's Katagi says the board split sharpens if it persists. The second estimate is 8 Dec, after the MoF corporate survey on capex.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no growth","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Missed; consumption −0.3% q/q, capex +1.6%","src":"Cabinet Office ESRI first preliminary (16 Nov) via Admiral Markets, Seoul Economic Daily and Yahoo Finance; Mace News consensus","trend":"down","trendWord":"First fall in 4 quarters","url":"https://admiralmarkets.com/analytics/traders-blog/japan-gdp-q3-2026-yen-usdjpy-160"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.5},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.4},{"l":"May 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"2.2%","name":"Core CPI (ex fresh food), y/y","next":"18 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"2.1%","read":"October core CPI rose 2.2% from 2.1%, under the 2.3% consensus (Reuters poll of 16; QUICK median 2.3%) and the highest since December 2025, a second month at or above the 2% target. Headline was 2.3% (September 2.1%); food inflation slowed to 2.2% from 2.9% while goods rose 3.2% (2.8%) and services 1.4%, so the mix is energy and goods rather than demand. With September real wages at +2.2% the reading is at target rather than squeezing consumption, and it leaves December intact: Minami (Norinchukin) expects a hike next month and another in March 2027 when inflation is likely to exceed 3%; Shinke (Dai-ichi Life) says only market instability could make the Bank stand pat. Tokyo core for November printed 2.9% on 27 Nov (energy +9.5% after the subsidies ended, core-core 2.5%), so the national November core due 18 Dec at 08:30, the morning of the BoJ decision, is likely to print above 2.2% on energy; consensus is not yet published.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoJ target","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"At target; missed 2.3%","src":"MIC via Reuters (Investing.com, AOL), Trading Economics, ChainCatcher (headline), Fisco via Zaikei (QUICK median)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Highest since Dec 2025","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"−0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.9},{"l":"May 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"2.2%","name":"Core-core CPI (ex food and energy), y/y","next":"18 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"2.4%","read":"Core-core CPI eased to 2.2% from September's 2.4%, a six-month high, so the demand-driven measure the BoJ watches is back at target. Goods prices rose 3.2% y/y (2.8% in September) and services 1.4% (1.5%), so the acceleration is in goods, food ex fresh slowed to 2.2% from 2.9%, and services inflation is still short of 2%. Tokyo core-core eased to 2.5% in November from 2.6% in October, so the demand measure is cooling in the early read too. The print trims the overshoot case Ueda made on 19 Nov (underlying inflation 'could run above 2%') without removing December; the pace argument for 2027 is what it bears on.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2%","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"At target, services 1.4%","src":"MIC via Reuters (Investing.com, AOL), Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Eased from 6-month high","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"+0.3 pt","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.5},{"l":"May 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Nov 26","v":2.9}],"latest":"2.9%","name":"Tokyo Core CPI, y/y","next":"late Dec (Dec)","period":"Nov 2026","prior":"2.6%","read":"November core rose to 2.9% from 2.6%, the highest since July 2025 and a third month above 2%, against a 3.0% consensus (Reuters and QUICK medians; OANDA 2.9%), while headline rose to 2.8% and ex fresh food and energy eased to 2.5% from 2.6%. The driver is energy: +9.5% y/y from +2.2% as electricity (+9.5%, +14.2% m/m) and city gas (+13.7%, +13.0% m/m) lost the subsidy that still covered September usage in the October index, adding about 0.34 pt to the change in core; gasoline −1.1%, fresh food 1.8% from 8.6%, food ex fresh 3.1% (3.0%), eating out 4.7%, services 2.1% (2.2%), accommodation 3.6% (6.7%), rent 1.9%, non-Koshihikari rice −33.8%; core m/m +0.5%, core-core +0.1%. Above target on every measure, but the underlying pace cooled for a second month, which is why the yen and futures did not move (USD/JPY 159.26 to 159.28 in the minute after the release; JGB futures up to 128.35). National November CPI prints 18 Dec at 08:30, the morning of the BoJ decision; Middle East energy costs are the next upside input.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoJ target","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Energy-led; core-core eased","src":"MIC via OANDA Japan, investingLive, ActionForex and Trading Economics (27 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Highest since Jul 2025","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/tokyocpi-nov-2026-review/"},{"change":"0 bp (8–1)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":0.75},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.75},{"l":"May 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.25},{"l":"Oct 26","v":1.25},{"l":"26 Nov","v":1.25}],"latest":"1.25%","name":"BoJ Policy Rate","next":"18 Dec","period":"Held 30 Oct; 2-year 1.740% on 26 Nov","prior":"1.25%","read":"Held at 1.25% on 30 Oct by 8–1: Takata dissented for 1.50%. The inflation side firmed again on 27 Nov: Tokyo November core 2.9% (consensus 3.0%), a third month above 2%, with core-core easing to 2.5%; October national core was 2.2% and services PPI +4.2% y/y, the fastest since 1990. Tamura (Kofu, 26 Nov) said the rate must reach neutral (about 2%) soon, that upside price signs warrant \"accelerating the pace of policy interest rate hikes\", that 25 bp every few months is his base case and \"a 50-basis-point rate hike may become necessary\" if upside risks persist, that a hike will be discussed \"at the December or a later meeting\" with a good chance within about three months of September, and that the AI-led equity correction can affect corporate and household decisions. The market still prices part of a hold: MogWatch OIS 74.7% for 18 Dec at 00:10 JST on 27 Nov, before the CPI (76.7% on 26 Nov, 84.7% on 20 Nov), implied 1.437% for December, 1.481% for January and 1.595% for March, with 1.75% or higher by March at 43.2%; the Reuters poll 86% of economists. The 2-year closed 1.740% on 26 Nov (+1.5 bp), still under the 1.75% line, and the BoJ 10- to 25-year operation drew 4.43 cover, the highest since June, as investors sold into the Bank. The board is split on the end-point: Tamura about 2%, Takata the middle of 1.1% to 2.5%, Koeda clearly above 1%, Asada and Sato limited room; Ueda speaks in Nagoya on 30 Nov. Under the framework the stance is Tightening with the pace disputed between the market and the hawks: a hike on 18 Dec to 1.50% is the base case, and the equity drawdown and super-long demand are the two things that could hold it; a 50 bp move is Tamura's view, not the board's.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Tightening; Dec pricing about 75%","src":"Bank of Japan (schedule, speeches page); Reuters via MarketScreener and Jiji via nippon.com (Tamura press conference); MogWatch (OIS 27 Nov 00:10 JST); QUICK/NQN via SMTB (2-year, BoJ operation); OANDA Japan and investingLive (Tokyo CPI); Reuters via Investing.com (poll)","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Hawkish hold","url":"https://mogwatch.jp/boj"},{"change":"+¥1.2tn in Oct","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":697.9},{"l":"Dec 25","v":677.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":682.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":683.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":663},{"l":"Apr 26","v":663.3},{"l":"May 26","v":664.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":639.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":644.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":644.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":625},{"l":"Oct 26","v":626.2}],"latest":"¥626tn","name":"BoJ Total Assets","next":"early Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"¥625tn","read":"Total assets were ¥626.2tn at 31 Oct (BoJ accounts, published 4 Nov), up ¥1.2tn from ¥625.0tn at 30 Sep, with JGB holdings ¥505.3tn (+¥1.3tn): purchases of about ¥2.3tn a month this quarter outran light October redemptions. The balance sheet is down from a ¥765tn peak in Aug 2024 as JGB purchases are cut by about ¥200bn a quarter, heading to ¥2tn a month from April 2027; the June plan targets JGB holdings of about ¥480tn by March 2027. Quarter-end months dip on redemptions (September −¥20tn), so the October uptick is the between-quarter pattern, not a change in policy; ETF sales of about ¥860bn a year start in January.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"QT by taper","src":"Bank of Japan accounts (31 Oct and 30 Sep 2026); BoJ purchase plan","trend":"up","trendWord":"Flat after Q3 dip","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/statistics/boj/other/acmai/release/2026/ac261031.htm"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the leading and coincident core. They are not part of the original indicator list.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"+2.5 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.06},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.24},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.11},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.345},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.515},{"l":"May 26","v":2.65},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.67},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.79},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.94},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.063},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.77},{"l":"26 Nov","v":2.645}],"latest":"2.645%","name":"10-Year JGB Yield","period":"26 Nov close","prior":"2.620% on 25 Nov (QUICK/NQN revised; 2.645% recorded)","read":"The 10-year closed 26 Nov at 2.645% (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, +2.5 bp against a revised 2.620% for 25 Nov; the SMTB table shows 2.635% and Fisco 2.615%, so the sources span 3 bp), after a 2.660% high in the morning as the Nikkei rose 1,240 points and Tamura spoke; December futures closed 128.24, unchanged, in a 127.96 to 128.41 range. The curve twisted: the 2-year rose 1.5 bp and the 5-year 2.5 bp on the hawkish speech while the 20-year fell 2 bp, the 30-year 5.5 bp and the 40-year 6.5 bp after the BoJ 10- to 25-year purchase operation drew 4.43 cover (3.58 last time, highest since June), which QUICK read as investors selling into the Bank. On 27 Nov futures opened 128.23 (−1 sen) and traded to 128.35 after the Tokyo core CPI printed 2.9% against 3.0%; European yields rose on 26 Nov with the US closed. Under the framework the 10-year sits near the bottom of its November range (2.62% to 2.92%) with the haven bid fading as equities recover; the 10-year auction on 1 Dec and Ueda on 30 Nov are the next tests, and the US 10-year (4.85% on 25 Nov) reopens for a half-session on 27 Nov.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Haven bid fading","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB (2.645%, +2.5 bp, high 2.660%, curve, futures, BoJ operation); SMTB market table (2.635%); Fisco via Zaikei 879296 (2.615%, futures range); QUICK/NQN via SMTB 27 Nov 09:06 (futures open)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"change":"+1.5 bp","hist":[{"l":"9 Nov","v":1.744},{"l":"10 Nov","v":1.761},{"l":"11 Nov","v":1.855},{"l":"13 Nov","v":1.88},{"l":"16 Nov","v":1.87},{"l":"17 Nov","v":1.87},{"l":"18 Nov","v":1.86},{"l":"19 Nov","v":1.845},{"l":"20 Nov","v":1.82},{"l":"24 Nov","v":1.765},{"l":"25 Nov","v":1.72},{"l":"26 Nov","v":1.74}],"latest":"1.740%","name":"2-Year JGB Yield","period":"26 Nov close","prior":"1.725% on 25 Nov (QUICK/NQN revised; 1.720% recorded)","read":"The 2-year closed 26 Nov at 1.740% (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, +1.5 bp; Fisco 1.726%) with the 5-year at 2.055% (+2.5 bp) after Tamura said the pace of hikes may need to accelerate and a 50 bp move may become necessary; it is still under 1.75%, so part of a hold is priced. MogWatch OIS: 74.7% for 18 Dec at 00:10 JST on 27 Nov, before the CPI (76.7% on 26 Nov, 84.7% on 20 Nov), implied 1.437% for December, 1.481% for January, 1.595% for March, 1.75% or higher by March 43.2% (45% on 26 Nov); the Reuters poll 86% of economists for 1.50%. The Tokyo core CPI at 2.9% against 3.0% on 27 Nov lifted futures 12 sen at most and the yen not at all; no 27 Nov cash close was available at this refresh. Under the framework a close under 1.75% says the market is not fully with the Bank, and the conflict is now between a member talking about 50 bp and OIS pricing three-quarters of 25 bp: Ueda on 30 Nov and the ¥2.8tn 2-year auction the same day decide it; a close back above 1.85% reprices December, under 1.70% says the hold is the base case.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Hold still part-priced","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB (1.740%, +1.5 bp); Fisco via Zaikei 879296 (1.726%); MogWatch (OIS 27 Nov 00:10 JST); Reuters via MarketScreener (Tamura); OANDA Japan (Tokyo CPI reaction)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Tamura","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"change":"−5.5 bp","hist":[{"l":"9 Nov","v":3.85},{"l":"10 Nov","v":3.865},{"l":"11 Nov","v":3.94},{"l":"13 Nov","v":4},{"l":"16 Nov","v":4.03},{"l":"17 Nov","v":4.04},{"l":"18 Nov","v":4.045},{"l":"19 Nov","v":3.99},{"l":"20 Nov","v":3.975},{"l":"24 Nov","v":3.94},{"l":"25 Nov","v":3.95},{"l":"26 Nov","v":3.895}],"latest":"3.895%","name":"30-Year JGB Yield","period":"26 Nov close","prior":"3.950% on 25 Nov (QUICK/NQN)","read":"The 30-year closed 26 Nov at 3.895% (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, −5.5 bp) and the 40-year at 4.115% (−6.5 bp) with the 20-year at 3.560% (−2 bp), reversing the 25 Nov sell-off that followed the 40-year auction (4.09% on 2.63 cover), while the 2- to 10-year sector rose 1.5 to 2.5 bp on Tamura: the BoJ 10- to 25-year purchase operation drew 4.43 cover against 3.58, the highest since June, which QUICK read as heavy investor selling into the Bank rather than fresh demand. The MoF primary-dealer meeting on 2027 issuance (26 Nov) heard requests for increases and decreases by maturity (QUICK headline; no readout in the pages opened), and the debut 2-year floating-rate bond planned for January was shelved pending demand (Reuters, 17 Nov); the investor meeting is 27 Nov and the plan is due at year-end with a June review; MUFG expects total issuance to rise by the low ¥10tns in FY2027. Under the framework a super-long rally on central-bank absorption is not a change in the fiscal signal, and with the yen at 159.25 the warn pair (super-long yields with a weak yen) stays on; a 30-year close above 4.05% while equities fall is the term-premium signal, 4.30% the fiscal line, and a close under 3.90% with USD/JPY under 158.5 turns the pair off (the first half is met, the second is not). The next 30-year auction is 8 Dec (MUFG).","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Rally on BoJ op; pair on","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB (30-year 3.895%, 40-year 4.115%, 20-year 3.560%, BoJ operation cover, 25 Nov auction); QUICK Money World headline (PD meeting); Reuters via MarketScreener and FixedIncomeNews (floating-rate bond); MUFG weekly (30-year auction date)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Op bid","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"change":"+0.86%","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":50339.48},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53322.85},{"l":"Feb 26","v":58850.27},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51063.72},{"l":"Apr 26","v":59284.92},{"l":"May 26","v":66329.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":70062.32},{"l":"Jul 26","v":64362.02},{"l":"Aug 26","v":66311.93},{"l":"Sep 26","v":66753.72},{"l":"Oct 26","v":63290.11},{"l":"26 Nov","v":57499.16}],"latest":"57,499.16","name":"Nikkei 225","period":"26 Nov close","prior":"57,011.62 on 25 Nov","read":"The Nikkei closed 26 Nov at 57,499.16 (+487.54, +0.86%) after opening 57,429.54, rising more than 1,240 to 58,251.93 before noon and fading to 57,288.10 in the afternoon; Prime turnover ¥6.83tn on 2,242m shares. The gain was the AI names in reverse: Advantest 24,830 (+1,085, +234 index points), SoftBank Group 2,894 (+7.3%, +160), Kioxia 7,762 (+12.6%, +61), TDK +34, Tokyo Electron +23, Ibiden +21, Lasertec +17; Fast Retailing 85,500 (−670, −54), Daikin −18, Chugai −12, Sony −11; the two leaders were about 393 of the 488 points. Breadth was flat (749 up, 751 down; 17 of 33 sectors up) as the defensives bought on 25 Nov were sold. Nikkei VI closed 31.13 (−2.26) after a 43.22 intraday high; December futures 57,350 (+310), night session 57,890 (+540, Nikkei headline); Growth 250 652.60 (+12.00). Kabutan cites a report that Nvidia asked partners to halt server shipments to four Southeast Asian countries and the December Huang testimony for the fade. The close is 21.0% under the 72,831.73 record, 1,063 points above the 20 Nov liquidation close (56,436.65) and 4.2% under the 60,030 first-test line. Tokyo 27 Nov morning: 58,172.04 (+672.88, +1.17%) after a 57,669.79 open and a brief dip under the prior close, closing the morning near the high; Prime 1,164 up against 345 (75%), 29 of 33 sectors up; Kioxia, Lasertec and HOYA sharply higher; futures 58,250 (+900); turnover ¥3.44tn; Europe +1.6% to +2.1% and US futures up on 26 Nov with the cash market closed. Under the framework the index is still an AI-complex trade, but the 27 Nov morning is the first session of the drawdown with the breadth and the index moving together to the upside; no new low, no stabilisation until 60,030.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Risk-off","src":"Kabutan n202611260984 (close, open, high, low, TOPIX, breadth, sectors, volume, Growth 250); Fisco via Zaikei 879302 (contributors) and 879295 (close); Gentosha Gold Online (turnover, movers); OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045983 (futures); Fisco via Investing.com 1771569 (Nikkei VI); Nikkei headline (night futures); OANDA Japan mn_1046143, Fisco via Zaikei 879444 and Reuters via Newsweek Japan (27 Nov morning)","trend":"up","trendWord":"AI names, faded","url":"https://s.kabutan.jp/news/n202611260984/"},{"change":"+0.11%","hist":[{"l":"Dec 24","v":2784.92},{"l":"Mar 25","v":2658.73},{"l":"Jun 25","v":2852.84},{"l":"Sep 25","v":3137.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3408.97},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3497.86},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3994},{"l":"Sep 26","v":4108.65},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3965.07},{"l":"26 Nov","v":3750.4}],"latest":"3,750.40","name":"TOPIX","period":"26 Nov close","prior":"3,746.20 on 25 Nov","read":"TOPIX closed 26 Nov at 3,750.40 (+4.20, +0.11%) against the Nikkei +0.86%: Prime 749 up, 751 down, 17 of 33 sectors up with nonferrous metals, oil and coal, information and communication, glass and electrical equipment leading and air transport, food, pharmaceuticals, insurance and construction lagging, the mirror of 25 Nov; Growth 250 652.60 (+12.00). On 27 Nov it was 3,809.02 (+58.62, +1.56%) at the morning close, outpacing the Nikkei (+1.17%), with 1,164 Prime names up against 345 (75%) and 29 of 33 sectors up. The 26 Nov close was 3.7% under the 3,895 first-test line, 8.7% under the 4,108.65 September close and 76 points above the 20 Nov low close of 3,674.22; the morning level is 2.2% from 3,895. Under the framework TOPIX up 1.56% on 75% breadth is the first broad up-session of the drawdown, and it is still short of the line.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Risk-off","src":"Kabutan n202611260984 and Fisco via Zaikei 879295 (26 Nov close, breadth, sectors); OANDA Japan mn_1046143 and Reuters via Newsweek Japan (27 Nov morning); kabutore.biz quarterly closes","trend":"up","trendWord":"Flat breadth","url":"https://s.kabutan.jp/news/n202611260984/"},{"change":"+0.08","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":156.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":154.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":155.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":159.8},{"l":"Apr 26","v":160.2},{"l":"May 26","v":159.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":161.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":160.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":160.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":157.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":157.55},{"l":"26 Nov","v":159.25}],"latest":"159.25","name":"USD/JPY","period":"26 Nov (New York close)","prior":"159.17 on 25 Nov","read":"USD/JPY closed 26 Nov in New York at 159.25 (Gaitame; +0.08 from 159.17) in a holiday session with a 16-sen range: 159.29 at 02:00 JST and 159.27 at 04:00 after a 159.34 high at the London fix, with the day low 158.95 in Tokyo when Tamura spoke at 10:00; EUR/JPY 174.70 (174.37 to 174.93), EUR/USD 1.0969. Tokyo 27 Nov: 159.27 at the 08:30 Tokyo CPI and 159.26 to 159.28 in the minute after it, a 159.38 high before 10:00, 159.19 at noon in a 159.14 to 159.38 range with the Nikkei up 1.2% and after-hours US yields higher; EUR/JPY 174.80, EUR/USD 1.0980. No MoF or Katayama comment was found; Gaitame flags 159.5 to 160 as the intervention-watch zone, month-end fixing flows at 01:00 JST and a Black Friday half-session with thin liquidity. The Fed: CME 97.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec; the US 2-year was 4.61% and the 10-year 4.85% at the 25 Nov close. Under the framework the pair is in the intervention zone with the front-end gap about 287 bp (US 2-year 4.61%, JGB 1.740%), a Tokyo core CPI at 2.9% and a member talking about 50 bp did not move it, and the haven bid shows only on broad US falls; 160 is 75 sen away and the carry-unwind path (155 to 156) needs the equity fall to spread beyond the AI names, which the 27 Nov breadth says it is not doing.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Near 160","src":"Gaitame (159.25 close, 159.34 high, 27 Nov outlook); Myforex DZH dzh261127015 and dzh261127028 (New York 2:00 and 4:00 JST, range 158.95 to 159.34, EUR/JPY, EUR/USD); OANDA Japan mn_1045961 (Tokyo 15:00), tokyocpi-nov-2026-review (08:30 reaction) and mn_1046144 (27 Nov noon)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Flat","url":"https://www.gaitame.com/media/entry/2026/11/27/101539"}],"sub":"Additions to the leading and coincident core. Closing levels for Wednesday 4 Nov unless a date is shown; 5 Nov figures are intraday.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["Leading indicators: the September leading index was revised up to 116.5 from 116.4 and the coincident index held at 119.5 (Cabinet Office, 26 Nov). The November flash PMIs stand: manufacturing 53.7 (October 53.3, consensus 53.5), output 53.8, export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018; services 50.5 (51.7) with new business at a four-month low; composite 51.5, a six-month low. Standing: BoJ Tankan +24, Reuters Tankan +27 and +35, core machinery orders +13.4% y/y, consumer confidence 35.0, housing starts −2.7%. Housing starts for October print 30 Nov, the manufacturing final 1 Dec, consumer confidence 2 Dec, the services final 3 Dec.","Coincident data: no new print. October exports +20.5% y/y to a record ¥11,768.8bn; Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized (consumption −0.3% q/q, capex +1.6%; second estimate 8 Dec); industrial production +1.7% y/y; real wages +2.2% (nominal +4.6%); unemployment 2.4%; jobs ratio 1.19; retail sales +2.3%. Chief Cabinet Secretary Kihara (26 Nov) called Japan's inclusion in the USTR Section 301 proposal regrettable, said Japan has no structural overcapacity or non-market practices, that this had been conveyed to Washington, and that the US had agreed not to impose tariffs beyond the 2025 deal, which the 15% total rate with autos, steel and semiconductors exempt reflects; the 20% alternative, comments to 5 Jan and the hearing from 25 Jan 2027 stand. October industrial production, labour, retail and housing starts print 30 Nov with the November intervention total. With core CPI at 2.2% and real wages at +2.2% the virtuous-cycle test is still passed.","Prices and policy: Tokyo November CPI (27 Nov, 08:30): core 2.9% y/y from 2.6%, the highest since July 2025 and a third month above 2%, against a 3.0% consensus (Reuters and QUICK; OANDA 2.9%); headline 2.8%; core-core 2.5% from 2.6%; energy +9.5% from 2.2% (electricity +9.5%, city gas +13.7%, gasoline −1.1%) as the electricity and gas subsidies ended, adding about 0.34 pt to the core change; fresh food 1.8% from 8.6%; food ex fresh 3.1% (3.0%), eating out 4.7%, services 2.1% (2.2%), accommodation 3.6% (6.7%), rent 1.9%; core m/m +0.5%, core-core m/m +0.1%. Tamura (Kofu, 26 Nov): the rate must reach neutral (about 2%) soon, upside price signs warrant \"accelerating the pace of policy interest rate hikes\", 25 bp every few months is the base case and \"a 50-basis-point rate hike may become necessary\" if upside risks persist; a hike will be discussed \"at the December or a later meeting\" with a good chance within about three months of September; the AI-led equity correction can affect corporate and household decisions. MogWatch OIS: 74.7% for 18 Dec at 00:10 JST on 27 Nov (76.7% on 26 Nov, 84.7% on 20 Nov), implied 1.437%, January 1.481%, March 1.595%, 1.75% or higher by March 43.2%; the Reuters poll 86%. JGBs 26 Nov (QUICK/NQN): 2-year 1.740% (+1.5 bp), 5-year 2.055% (+2.5 bp), 10-year 2.645% (+2.5 bp, high 2.660%), 20-year 3.560% (−2 bp), 30-year 3.895% (−5.5 bp), 40-year 4.115% (−6.5 bp), futures 128.24 (unchanged); the BoJ 10- to 25-year operation drew 4.43 cover (3.58), the 3- to 5-year 2.51, which QUICK read as investors selling into the Bank. The MoF primary-dealer meeting (26 Nov) heard calls for increases and decreases by maturity (QUICK headline) and the debut 2-year floating-rate bond planned for January was shelved pending demand (Reuters, 17 Nov); the investor meeting is 27 Nov. Futures opened 27 Nov at 128.23 (−1 sen) and traded to 128.35 after the CPI miss. The Fed: CME 97.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec; the US was closed 26 Nov.","Yen and oil: USD/JPY closed 26 Nov in New York at 159.25 (Gaitame; +0.08 from 159.17), after a 158.95 Tokyo low when Tamura spoke and a 159.34 high after the London fix in a 16-sen New York range on the holiday; EUR/JPY 174.70, EUR/USD 1.0969. Tokyo 27 Nov: 159.27 at the 08:30 release, 159.26 to 159.28 in the minute after it, a 159.38 high before 10:00, 159.19 at noon in a 159.14 to 159.38 range with the Nikkei up 1.2% and after-hours US yields higher; EUR/JPY 174.80, EUR/USD 1.0980. No MoF or Katayama comment was found; Gaitame flags 159.5 to 160 as the intervention-watch zone, month-end London fixing flows at 01:00 JST and a Black Friday half-session. Oil and gold on 26 Nov were intraday only in the pages opened (WTI January $83.94, +0.31%; Brent $94.48, −0.14%; gold $3,562, +0.74%, at 08:30 GMT); OPEC+ meets 29 Nov.","Markets: Tokyo 26 Nov close: Nikkei 57,499.16 (+487.54, +0.86%), open 57,429.54, high 58,251.93, low 57,288.10, Prime turnover ¥6.83tn on 2,242m shares, 749 up against 751 down, 17 of 33 sectors up (nonferrous metals, oil and coal, information and communication, glass, electrical equipment) against air transport, food, pharmaceuticals, insurance and construction; Advantest 24,830 (+1,085, +4.6%, +234 points), SoftBank Group 2,894 (+197.5, +7.3%, +160), Kioxia 7,762 (+867, +12.6%, +61), TDK +34, Tokyo Electron 7,871 (+46, +23), Ibiden +21, Lasertec +17; Fast Retailing 85,500 (−670, −54 points), Daikin −18, Chugai −12, Sony −11; the two leaders were about 393 of the 488 points; December futures 57,350 (+310), night session 57,890 (+540, Nikkei headline); Nikkei VI 31.13 (−2.26) after 43.22 intraday; Growth 250 652.60 (+12.00). Kabutan cites the Nvidia report on halting server shipments to four Southeast Asian countries and the December Huang testimony for the afternoon fade; Fisco cites dividend reinvestment and Tamura. Tokyo 27 Nov morning: Nikkei 58,172.04 (+672.88, +1.17%), open 57,669.79, a brief dip under 57,499 then a close near the high; TOPIX 3,809.02 (+58.62, +1.56%); Prime 1,164 up, 345 down, 40 flat (75% advancing), 29 of 33 sectors up led by nonferrous metals, oil and machinery against services, land transport and retail; Kioxia, Lasertec, HOYA (upgrade) sharply higher, Advantest, SoftBank Group, Tokyo Electron, Ibiden, Murata and Fujikura up, Recruit, Fast Retailing, Sony and Nitori down; futures 58,250 (+900); turnover ¥3.44tn on 993m shares; FTSE +1.75%, DAX +2.08%, CAC +1.61% on 26 Nov. The first stabilisation test (60,030, 3,895, VI under 35) was 4.2% and 3.7% away at the 26 Nov close and 3.2% and 2.2% away at the morning levels, with the VI now under 35."],"translation":[{"body":"The 26 Nov close (Nikkei 57,499.16, +0.86%; TOPIX 3,750.40, +0.11%) was the third session in which four names set the index: Advantest, SoftBank Group and Kioxia added about 455 points and a 1,240-point morning gain faded to 488 on an Nvidia shipment report, with Prime breadth flat at 749 against 751 as the defensives bought on 25 Nov were sold. The 27 Nov morning is different in kind: 58,172.04 (+1.17%) with TOPIX +1.56%, 75% of Prime up and 29 of 33 sectors rising, the first session of the drawdown in which the index and the market rose together. Under the framework the drawdown is 21.0% from the record on the 26 Nov close, the 20 Nov liquidation low (56,436.65) is 1,063 points below, the Nikkei VI (31.13) is back under 35, and the posture stays Risk-off until a close retakes 60,030 and 3,895, which the morning levels miss by 3.2% and 2.2%. The Section 301 risk is unchanged: Kihara protested and says 15% matches the deal; the 20% alternative is the tail.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"JGBs twisted on 26 Nov: the 2-year rose 1.5 bp to 1.740% and the 5-year 2.5 bp on Tamura, the 10-year 2.5 bp to 2.645%, while the 20-year fell 2 bp, the 30-year 5.5 bp to 3.895% and the 40-year 6.5 bp to 4.115% after the BoJ 10- to 25-year operation drew 4.43 cover, the highest since June; futures closed 128.24 unchanged and opened 27 Nov at 128.23, trading to 128.35 after Tokyo core CPI printed 2.9% against 3.0%. The read under the framework: the super-long rally is investors selling into the Bank, not demand, so the fiscal signal is unchanged and the warn pair (super-long yields with a weak yen) stays on with USD/JPY at 159.25; the 2-year under 1.75% says the market still prices part of a hold (MogWatch 74.7% for December, March implied 1.595%) on the morning a board member said 50 bp may become necessary; and the Tokyo CPI gave both sides a number, core 2.9% on energy for the hawks and core-core 2.5% for the market. The yen did not move on either (159.26 to 159.28 after the release, 159.19 at noon), the front-end gap is about 287 bp, and 160 is 75 sen away with no MoF comment since 19 Nov; the 2-year auction, Ueda in Nagoya and the intervention total on 30 Nov are the next tests.","title":"Fixed income and currency"},{"body":"The BoJ is tightening into an equity drawdown of 21.0% from the record with the early inflation read above target (Tokyo core 2.9%, core-core 2.5%; national core 2.2%; services PPI +4.2%), real wages positive (+2.2%) and the composite PMI at a six-month low (51.5). Tamura wants the rate at neutral (about 2%) soon, 25 bp every few months as the base case and 50 bp if prices overshoot, with December or a later meeting the venue; the market prices 74.7% for December and 1.595% by March. The Fed is at 97.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec after core PCE 3.3% and Q3 GDP 4.3%, so a BoJ hike to 1.50% leaves the gap unchanged and the pace difference is what the yen trades. The Bank absorbed heavy selling in its 10- to 25-year operation (4.43 cover) on the day the MoF heard dealers' requests for the 2027 issuance plan and after it shelved the debut 2-year floater for want of demand; the investor meeting is 27 Nov, the plan is due at year-end, MUFG expects issuance up by the low ¥10tns, and the food consumption-tax cut (about ¥5tn a year, unfunded) is in the Diet to 12 Dec. The USTR Section 301 proposal is a political item after Kihara's protest. The balance sheet (¥626.2tn) is unchanged; the taper continues toward ¥2tn a month of JGB purchases from April 2027.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
